{"name":"Silica","hex":"#dbd4bf","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silica","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Silicon dioxide. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Any of the silica group of the silicate minerals."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silica"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silica (video game)","blocks":[{"text":"Silica is a 2023 science fiction real-time strategy shooter video game developed by Martin \"Dram\" Melichárek and published by Bohemia Interactive, released for Microsoft Windows in early access exclusively through the Steam distribution platform. Set in the 24th century on the exoplanet Baltarus, Silica follows a three-sided resource war over Balterium, a valuable mineral capable of power manipulation. The game features a hybrid of shooter gameplay and strategy gameplay, with both modes accessible based on the player's role in their faction."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/7c/Silica-cover.jpg/330px-Silica-cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silica_(video_game)","wikidata":"Q118698046"},{"title":"Silicon dioxide","blocks":[{"text":"Silicon dioxide, also known as silica, is an oxide of silicon with the chemical formula SiO2, and is commonly found in nature as quartz. In many parts of the world, silica is the major constituent of sand. Silica is one of the most complex and abundant families of materials, existing as a compound of several minerals and as a synthetic product. Examples include fused quartz, fumed silica, opal, and aerogels. It is used in structural materials, microelectronics, and as components in the food and pharmaceutical industries. All forms are white or colorless, although impure samples can be colored or opaque."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Sample_of_silicon_dioxide.jpg/330px-Sample_of_silicon_dioxide.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silicon_dioxide","wikidata":"Q116269"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/SiO2repeat.png/500px-SiO2repeat.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/SiO2repeat.png/500px-SiO2repeat.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Structural motif found in α-quartz, but also found in almost all forms of silicon dioxide","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASiO2repeat.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Quartzrn.PNG/500px-Quartzrn.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Quartzrn.PNG/1280px-Quartzrn.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Quartzrn.PNG/500px-Quartzrn.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Relationship between refractive index and density for some SiO2 forms[7]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AQuartzrn.PNG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Kieselsaeure380m2prog.jpg/500px-Kieselsaeure380m2prog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Kieselsaeure380m2prog.jpg/500px-Kieselsaeure380m2prog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Manufactured fumed silica with maximum surface area of 380 m2/g","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKieselsaeure380m2prog.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Fibreoptic.jpg/330px-Fibreoptic.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail